            <ArticleTitle>Using Principles of Co-Production to Improve Patient Care and Enhance Value.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1125-1131</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">journalofethics.2017.19.11.pfor1-1711</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1001/journalofethics.2017.19.11.pfor1-1711</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Unlike goods, which are concrete and easily quantified, services are intangible processes that are produced and consumed concurrently. Health care is a service that can encourage optimal health outcomes only through meaningful, collaborative partnerships between patients and clinicians. Co-production of health services can be used as a means to rethink how health care is delivered not only in the context of face-to-face encounters in which the benefits of working together are obvious, but also in designing systems that can improve patient care and enhance value.</AbstractText>
